a why do you use shared jenkins libraries

Shared libraries are used to maintain reusable and standardized CI/CD logic across multiple applications and teams.

Instead of writing the same pipeline code in every project, common functionalities such as build logic, deployment logic, notifications, validations, and security checks are centralized inside shared libraries.

This reduces duplicate code, simplifies maintenance, and ensures all teams follow standard deployment practices.

b what is the purpose of yml file in your pipeline

YAML files are used to maintain project-specific configurations required for pipeline execution.

These configurations include details such as application ID, build type, builder node, deployment platform, branch-to-environment mapping, artifact details, and deployment environments.

The pipeline runner reads these YAML configurations and dynamically constructs the pipeline based on the deployment requirements.

c. how does jenkins securely connect on AWS

In our organization, we follow a passwordless authentication approach for Jenkins integrations.

Instead of storing static usernames and passwords inside pipelines, secure authentication methods such as IAM roles, service identities, tokens, or temporary credentials are used.

This allows Jenkins to securely interact with platforms such as AWS EKS, ECS, Lambda, Terraform, and JFrog while improving security and avoiding hardcoded credentials.

d. why do you store artifacts in jfrog
JFrog Artifactory is used as a centralized artifact and container image repository.

After the build process completes, generated JAR artifacts or Docker images are pushed to JFrog. Deployment systems then pull the required versions during deployments.

This helps maintain versioning, rollback capability, centralized artifact management, security control, and supports the build once deploy anywhere strategy.

e. difference between gitflow and trunkbased deployment

GitFlow is generally used for structured release management where separate feature, develop, release, and hotfix branches are maintained.

Trunk-based development focuses on frequent integration where developers merge smaller changes regularly into the main branch to support faster CI/CD and continuous delivery.

GitFlow is common in enterprise environments with controlled releases, while trunk-based development is preferred in fast-paced DevOps environments.


f. what happens if deployment fails in production

If a production deployment fails, we first analyze pipeline logs, deployment logs, and application logs to identify the root cause.

Depending on the issue, we either fix the configuration/problem and redeploy, or perform a rollback using the previously stable artifact or container image version available in JFrog Artifactory.

Since artifact versions are maintained centrally, rollback becomes faster and safer.

g. what is maven wrapper and why do we use it 

Maven Wrapper is a wrapper script used to maintain consistent builds across environments.

Instead of depending on globally installed Maven versions, the wrapper automatically uses the project-specific Maven version defined for the application.

This helps avoid version mismatch issues between developer systems and CI/CD environments such as Jenkins agents.

h. how are docker images created and deployed

For containerized applications, Docker images are created using Dockerfiles during the CI pipeline.

The application artifact such as a JAR file is packaged inside the Docker image. After the image is built and tagged, it is pushed to JFrog Artifactory.

Deployment platforms such as EKS or ECS then pull the required image version from JFrog and run the containerized application.


